This repository ("compile-time-browser" / ctbrowser) is a derivative
work built on:

    compile-time-javascript (ctjs)  https://github.com/alexios-angel/compile-time-javascript
    compile-time-css   (ctcss)  https://github.com/alexios-angel/compile-time-css

both of which historically consumed a compile-time-lark/CTLL grammar
layer descended from:

    notre (a fork of Compile Time Regular Expressions)
    https://github.com/alexios-angel/notre

which is itself a derivative of:

    Compile Time Regular Expressions (CTRE)
    https://github.com/hanickadot/compile-time-regular-expressions
    Copyright (c) Hana Dusikova and the CTRE contributors

License: Apache License 2.0, with LLVM Exceptions (see LICENSE in this
repository, reproduced unmodified from upstream).

What is derived
---------------
The two remaining bricks arrive as git submodules under third-party/ and
carry their own NOTICE files (historical CTLL/CTRE lineage, the
ECMA-262 and W3C CSS semantics; compile-time-containers, MIT, supplies
the old ctjs interpreter's containers in differential tests). This
repository began as a clone of
compile-time-css - the chain runs compile-time-xml ->
compile-time-html -> compile-time-css -> here - and keeps that git
history. Apart from the carried entity table described below and the
regular-expression matcher ported from ctjs, the DOM (its own WHATWG
tokenizer and tree builder), the style, layout, paint and raster
pipeline, the script VM and standard library, and the SDL3 shell are
original to this repository.

The named character reference data in
ctbrowser/include/ctbrowser/dom/entities.hpp was carried from
compile-time-html's include/cthtml/entities.hpp. It is the WHATWG HTML
Standard's named character references table
(https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/named-characters.html),
obtained there via CPython's html.entities module.

What an INSTALL of this package contains
----------------------------------------
Neither submodule's headers are installed, and no public ctbrowser
header names a ctjs or ctcss type. The engine uses
compile-time-javascript privately in the script compiler; tests also
use its parser directly, and use its old interpreter as a differential
oracle.

compile-time-css is used only by the opt-in bench_style differential
benchmark, which is not built by default and is never installed.

