--- the engine (ctbrowser/lib/) ---

The engine uses Boost (Boost Software License 1.0,
https://www.boost.org/LICENSE_1_0.txt) as an external dependency; nothing
from Boost is bundled.

HEADER-ONLY: Boost.Unordered (unordered_flat_map), Boost.Container
(small_vector, flat_map, stable_vector), Boost.Intrusive, Boost.SmartPtr
(intrusive_ptr), Boost.Lockfree (spsc_queue) and Boost.Asio.

LINKED: Boost.URL, since 2026-07-31. It is the first compiled Boost
library here and the restriction against them was lifted for it, because
there is no alternative: Boost.URL has been compiled-only since 1.87, its
`boost/url/src.hpp` now being a hard #error reading "src.hpp is
discontinued". The engine had two hand-written URL parsers that disagreed
with each other, and RFC 3986 is not worth hand-rolling a third time.

The restriction was never a preference - it was about the `windows` /
`windows-fetch` presets, which cross-compile with llvm-mingw, where a
header-only Boost needs no machinery and a compiled one must be built for
every target. That cost is now paid deliberately and in one place:
tools/mingw/build-boost-mingw.sh compiles Boost.URL's 66 sources with the cross
compiler into the mingw sysroot, beside the static SDL3 already there. It
does not use b2, so no Boost build system is involved.

Boost.Context (and therefore Boost.Coroutine2 and Boost.Fiber) is per-ABI
assembly and MUST STILL NOT be introduced. Lifting the rule for a library
that is portable C++ says nothing about one that is not.

NOT LINKED BY DEFAULT, AND THE ONLY DEPENDENCY HERE WHOSE LICENCE WOULD
CHANGE THE BINARY'S: GNU GMP, which is LGPLv3-or-later OR GPLv2-or-later.
`-DCTBROWSER_WITH_GMP=ON` makes it the Boost.Multiprecision backend for the
BigInt type; the default build does not reference it and needs it nowhere.

It is opt-in specifically BECAUSE of this paragraph rather than in spite of
it. This project is Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions and ships SELF-CONTAINED,
STATICALLY LINKED .exe files - no DLLs, deliberately (docs/platform.md).
Static linking against an LGPL library is permitted, but it carries
obligations the rest of this tree's dependencies do not: recipients must be
able to relink the work against a modified GMP, which in a static binary
means providing the objects or an equivalent mechanism, along with the LGPL
text and notice. Turning that on for anyone who merely happened to have GMP
installed - which is what "use it when available" would have meant - would
attach those obligations silently, to a binary whose whole distribution model
is a single file. Anyone enabling the option is taking on that duty
knowingly.

There is a second reason and it is not legal: GMP is SLOWER for this
workload. A JavaScript BigInt is an id, a timestamp or a 64-bit hash, and at
64 bits GMP measured 2.9x slower than the header-only cpp_int on Linux and
5.5x slower on Windows - every mpz_t being a heap allocation where cpp_int
keeps a small value inline. docs/script.md has the table and the method.

The one other LGPL item in this repository is p5.js (LGPL-2.1), and it
remains what it has always been: test data that nothing links.

The compositor's shaders (include/ctbrowser/gpu/shaders/tile.vert.glsl and
tile.frag.glsl) are original to this project. include/ctbrowser/gpu/shaders/tile_spv.hpp
is their SPIR-V, generated by tools/gen/gen-shaders.py using glslc from
Google's shaderc, which is a build-time tool and is NOT bundled or
linked.

--- the compiler (ctcompile/) ---

ctcompile uses LLVM and MLIR as external dependencies, pinned to 22.1.8
by cmake/LLVMVersion.cmake; nothing from either is bundled. They are
distributed under the Apache License 2.0 with LLVM Exceptions, which is
this repository's own licence.

TWO FILES ARE VENDORED FROM THEM, and they are the exception to the
sentence above:

    ctcompile/lib/Target/Cpp/TranslateToCpp.cpp
    ctcompile/include/ctcompile/Target/Cpp/CppEmitter.h

are a fork of mlir/lib/Target/Cpp/TranslateToCpp.cpp and
mlir/include/mlir/Target/Cpp/CppEmitter.h from llvm/llvm-project at tag
llvmorg-22.1.8, Copyright (c) the LLVM Project contributors. Each carries
its provenance, the upstream sha256 and the re-fetch command in its own
header comment, and the .cpp is in .clang-format-ignore so that the diff
against upstream stays readable. Same licence, so nothing changes about
what a binary built from this tree may be distributed under; the entry is
here because Section 4 of that licence asks for attribution and because
a fork that nobody records is a fork that stops being re-syncable.

ctcompile/test/Target/Cpp/upstream/ holds 35 of that project's own EmitC
lit tests, byte-for-byte from the same tag. They are test DATA on the
same terms as the JavaScript corpora above - nothing links, includes or
embeds them - and they are there to keep the fork a superset of what it
forked rather than a divergence from it.
